NEARS-3527 — security-reviewer qa_point: spot-check one legitimately-gated
module (wallet) via direct URL, employee who does/doesn't have it.

Fixtures created on the disposable QA clone (store_id=35, vendor_id=26):
  - EmployeeRole id=12 "QA-NoWallet-Role", modules=["order"]  (no wallet)
  - VendorEmployee id=12 qa.nowallet.3527@nears.com  -> employee_role_id=12
  - VendorEmployee id=13 qa.withwallet.3527@nears.com -> employee_role_id=10
    (role id=10 is the AC6 all-27-keys role, which includes "wallet")

Both logged in via /login/vendor-employee, role=vendor_employee, same curl
cookie-jar technique as the owner login.

GET /vendor-panel/wallet as qa.nowallet.3527@nears.com (role has NO 'wallet'):
-> HTTP 302 (redirected back by ModulePermissionMiddleware)
-> Following the redirect: toastr.error('Access denied')

GET /vendor-panel/wallet as qa.withwallet.3527@nears.com (role HAS 'wallet'):
-> HTTP 200 (page rendered, "wallet" appears 26x in the response body)

This confirms the middleware gate `module:wallet` is live and actually reads
EmployeeRole.modules — i.e. the modules[] array is a REAL privilege boundary,
which is exactly why this ticket's server-side whitelist on that array's
CONTENTS matters (an unguarded write path would let an employee escalate into
gates like this one).
